  "notes": "The UK's anti-formula pop factory: 116–146 straight with the backbeat on 2&4, \"with drum'n'bass/breakbeat inserts at double the grid\". That insert is the house transition device — \"a bar of breakbeat or drum'n'bass at 2× the grid\" — and it is authored as a 0.45 `switchUpProb` plus a 32nd-weighted roll vocabulary, which is what separates this model from the Girls Aloud artist entry that shares the same room. The other two deltas are quoted: \"gated 16th hats\" (a pinned 16th at a high fill density with a loud accent tier) and \"live-sounding toms\", the tom lanes leading the percs. The second house rule is a bass one and it is authored as an arrangement fact: \"a **one-bar total bass drop-out before the chorus** is the house lift\", which is `dropOutBeats` at 0.95 over 4 beats, and the bass itself is \"distorted saw playing a riff in 8ths with root-to-5th leaps\" — `independent_riff` at 0.5 with the fifth as its passing tone. Harmony is \"unstable by design — sections change harmonic centre; minor verses into non-resolving major choruses; songs can end on a chord other than the tonic. Two- and three-chord vamps with the bassline supplying the movement\", so `maxChords` is 3 and the families do not cadence. The documented team method — many separate hook cells bolted together, 4–6 melodic ideas per song, sections of 6, 10 or 12 bars — is the phrase weighting and the irregular `sectionBars`. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and says per-song harmonic claims are inferred. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and `bpm.mode` 132.",
